{
  "id": "Compound::DB00678 - Disease::DOID:10763",
  "verbalization": "Losartan, a well-known angiotensin II receptor antagonist, is implicated in the treatment of hypertension through its ability to block the action of angiotensin II, a hormone that causes vasoconstriction and increases blood pressure. This mechanism is supported by multiple pathways in the hetionet dataset, where Losartan is classified under the broader category of angiotensin II receptor antagonists, which include Telmisartan, Valsartan, and Irbesartan. All three of these drugs are directly linked to the treatment of hypertension, suggesting a consistent pharmacological relationship between the class of drugs and the disease. The lowest common ancestors of these drugs—Angiotensin II Receptor Antagonists and Organonitrogen Heterocyclic Compounds—highlight the biochemical commonality that underpins their therapeutic effects. The evidence from the dataset is robust, with a high final score, and supports the notion that Losartan, as part of this class, may be repurposed for the treatment of hypertension. The mechanism is mechanistically sound, and the evidence is consistent across multiple pathways, reinforcing the likelihood of a valid drug-repurposing relationship.",
